{
  "id": "T08",
  "title": "Monthly lookback windows across a leap day and the spring-forward DST change",
  "scenario": "A prior-authorization service checks whether a kidney-function result (eGFR) exists in a given calendar month. Results reach the server from reference labs and telehealth partners around the world, so offsets range from -12:00 to +14:00, including half- and three-quarter-hour offsets. The months involved are February 2024 (a leap year) and March 2024 (US daylight saving starts 2024-03-10).",
  "server_timezone": "America/New_York",
  "why_hard": "February 2024 local is [02-01T05:00Z, 03-01T05:00Z); March 2024 local is [03-01T05:00Z, 04-01T04:00Z) because DST starts mid-month. Every instant must be converted with its own offset (+14:00, +05:45, -03:30, -12:00) and compared with month bounds that use different offsets at each end. Values exactly on a boundary (04:59:59Z, 05:00:00Z) and the one-second width of a seconds-precision value decide eb/sa.",
